        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification \"Ms. Rivera has joined\" appears in the center of the screen. The tutor's icon, a red circle with the letter 'L', appears in the top right corner.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 205 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification \"Jordan has joined\" appears in the center of the screen. The student's icon, a green circle with the letter 'B', appears in the top right corner next to the tutor's icon.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 46 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE: Student leaves the session.] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Jordan has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 82 seconds]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: Hi.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 22 seconds]\nTurn 2. TUTOR: How's your summer been?\nTurn 3. STUDENT: Uh, it's been good.\nTurn 4. TUTOR: It's been good?\nTurn 5. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: What were you doing? Where'd you go? I know your mom said that you couldn't do tutoring 'cause you had vacations and trips planned.\nTurn 7. STUDENT: Uh, I had, um, summer camp. Then I hung out with my friends. Then, what else did I do? Then we went camping. And then, what did we do? We also celebrated my brother's birthday. And then we also, I think that's it.\nTurn 8. TUTOR: That's it? But it was just a busy month and the break was good, huh?\nTurn 9. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: Yeah.\nTurn 11. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: When does school start?\nTurn 13. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 13. TUTOR: I don't know what froze.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 14. TUTOR: When does school start?\nTurn 15. STUDENT: August twelfth.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: August twelfth.\nTurn 17. TUTOR: Oh my goodness. We have two weeks.\nTurn 18. STUDENT: There you go. I think it's lagging.\nTurn 19. STUDENT: Your face is frozen. Huh. I need to check my internet. Everything's lagging.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 21. STUDENT: Okay, okay. Good.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 22. TUTOR: A little better?\nTurn 23. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: Yeah? Okay.\nTurn 25. TUTOR: So, I heard that the, what is it? What were we talking about? The Summer I Turned Pretty?\nTurn 26. STUDENT: It was... I think it's... Wasn't it? I kinda forgot. Wait, hold on.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: Let me see.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds]\nTurn 28. STUDENT: Oh, My Life with the Walter Boys.\nTurn 29. TUTOR: Yes! Okay. There was a new season, I think, wasn't there?\nTurn 30. STUDENT: Uh, yeah, it's coming out. I bought the second book.\nTurn 31. TUTOR: You read the second book? And how was that? Was that any better?\nTurn 32. STUDENT: I bought it. I'm gonna read it soon.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: Oh, you just bought it. Okay. That's exciting.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Okay. Are you ready for seventh grade?\nTurn 35. STUDENT: No. Not quite.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: Yeah.\nTurn 37. TUTOR: Seventh grade is a fun year. I remember seventh grade being fun.\nTurn 38. TUTOR: I think it was... Yeah, I think seventh grade was more fun than sixth grade for me.\nTurn 39. TUTOR: More fun than sixth grade. Probably a little less fun than eighth grade. I think it was my, I think it was my favorite middle school year.\nTurn 40. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 41. TUTOR: For me at least, personally.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 43. TUTOR: All right. Sorry. What do you want to do? Do you want to work on math? Do you want to work on reading? I know we haven't had a session in a while.\nTurn 44. STUDENT: Um, math.\nTurn 45. TUTOR: Math? Okay. I want to meet you where you are. So,\nTurn 46. TUTOR: let me see.\nTurn 47. TUTOR: And then we've also been do- I've been introducing some more games with my students, not just an educational game platform. But like crosswords and word searches and uh, hangman and things like that when you guys want a break.\nTurn 48. STUDENT: Ooh.\nTurn 49. TUTOR: Yeah. So that way it's not just always an educational game platform, an educational game platform, an educational game platform.\nTurn 50. TUTOR: We will do just for fun...\nTurn 51. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The screen changes to a learning platform interface showing a list of topics.\nTurn 51. TUTOR: a question of the day.\nTurn 52. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 53. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: tutor - clicks] Tutor clicks the \"Resume\" button for \"Question of the Day\".\nTurn 53. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'What is the result of multiplying 3/4 by 3?' (start)] A multiple-choice question appears on a pink background. The question is \"What is the result of multiplying 3/4 by 3?\". The options are A) 6/4, B) 6/8, C) 1